{
  "id": "proj-garage-pegboard-wall",
  "slug": "garage-pegboard-wall",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Garage Pegboard Wall",
  "summary": "A framed pegboard wall with French-cleat shelves. Every tool gets a home and an outline.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 3,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Find the studs along an 8 ft run of wall with the stud finder and mark each one at 36 in and 84 in above the floor.",
    "Cut the 1x3 furring strips into two 96 in rails and three 43 in uprights. This frame holds the pegboard 3/4 in off the wall so the hooks have room to seat.",
    "Screw the bottom rail level at 36 in, then the top rail 48 in above it and the uprights between them, with two 2 1/2 in screws into every stud the frame crosses.",
    "Lift the pegboard onto the frame, check it is level, and fasten it every 12 in along the rails and uprights with 1 1/4 in screws and finish washers through the existing holes.",
    "Set the circular saw to a 45 degree bevel and rip one plywood panel into 4 in strips. Screw two strips end to end along the studs just above the pegboard, long edge up and the bevel sloping down toward the wall.",
    "From the second panel cut three 10 x 24 in shelves and three 5 1/2 x 24 in back plates. Glue and screw each back plate under the back edge of its shelf, then screw a 24 in cleat strip to the back plate with its bevel facing down and in, so it hooks over the wall cleat.",
    "Sand the cut edges, hang the shelves, and load the pegboard with hooks. Hang each tool, trace its outline with the paint marker, and it always goes back to its own shape."
  ],
